window.MA_DATA = {"arts": [{"id": "01-taekwondo", "flag": "🇰🇷", "n": "Taekwondo", "c": "Korea", "les": "15", "multi": "3-step sparring, roundhouse vs back-kick", "range": "Long", "weap": "Kicks", "ground": "—", "sport": "Olympic sparring, poomsae", "best": "Speed, flexibility, kids"}, {"id": "02-silat-gayong", "flag": "🇲🇾", "n": "Silat Seni Gayong", "c": "Malaysia", "les": "10", "multi": "Lock & takedown, keris disarm, 2 attackers (3 people)", "range": "All", "weap": "Strikes, locks, weapons", "ground": "Takedowns", "sport": "Silat olahraga (some)", "best": "Complete traditional system"}, {"id": "03-silat-cekak", "flag": "🇲🇾", "n": "Silat Cekak", "c": "Malaysia", "les": "7", "multi": "vs punch, grab, kick, knife, rear hug", "range": "Close", "weap": "Parry-strike, locks", "ground": "Takedowns", "sport": "—", "best": "Fast practical self-defence"}, {"id": "04-pencak-silat", "flag": "🇮🇩", "n": "Pencak Silat", "c": "Indonesia", "les": "8", "multi": "Tanding catch-sweep, guntingan, ganda golok", "range": "Mid", "weap": "Kicks, sweeps, catches", "ground": "Takedowns", "sport": "Tanding, tunggal, ganda", "best": "Sport + culture"}, {"id": "05-karate", "flag": "🇯🇵", "n": "Karate", "c": "Japan", "les": "8", "multi": "Ippon kumite, WKF exchange", "range": "Mid", "weap": "Punches, kicks", "ground": "—", "sport": "Kata, kumite", "best": "Power, discipline"}, {"id": "06-judo", "flag": "🇯🇵", "n": "Judo", "c": "Japan", "les": "9", "multi": "Kuzushi, o-goshi, seoi-nage, o-soto-gari, de-ashi-barai, kesa-gatame", "range": "Clinch", "weap": "Throws, pins", "ground": "Yes", "sport": "Olympic", "best": "Throws, falling safely"}, {"id": "07-wing-chun", "flag": "🇨🇳", "n": "Wing Chun", "c": "China", "les": "8", "multi": "Poon sau, lap sau (+ wooden dummy)", "range": "Close", "weap": "Punches, traps", "ground": "—", "sport": "—", "best": "Efficient close range"}, {"id": "08-muay-thai", "flag": "🇹🇭", "n": "Muay Thai", "c": "Thailand", "les": "9", "multi": "Pads, kick check, plum clinch", "range": "Mid–clinch", "weap": "8 limbs", "ground": "—", "sport": "Ring fights", "best": "Hard striking, fitness"}, {"id": "09-bjj", "flag": "🇧🇷", "n": "Brazilian Jiu-Jitsu", "c": "Brazil", "les": "8", "multi": "Guard, armbar, sweep, pass, escape, RNC", "range": "Ground", "weap": "Chokes, joint locks", "ground": "Core", "sport": "Gi / no-gi", "best": "Ground control, size gaps"}, {"id": "10-aikido", "flag": "🇯🇵", "n": "Aikido", "c": "Japan", "les": "8", "multi": "Ikkyō, shihō-nage, kote-gaeshi, irimi-nage, randori vs 3 (4 people)", "range": "Mid", "weap": "Blending, locks, throws", "ground": "Pins", "sport": "(Tomiki only)", "best": "Lifelong, cooperative"}], "md": {"01-taekwondo": "# 01 · Taekwondo (태권도) — \"The Way of the Foot and Fist\"\n\n> **Origin:** Korea · **Type:** striking, kick-dominant · **Governing bodies:** World Taekwondo (WT) & Kukkiwon; International Taekwon-Do Federation (ITF) · **Olympic:** since Sydney 2000\n\n---\n\n## 1. History & background\n\n| Period | What happened |\n|---|---|\n| Ancient Korea | Native foot-fighting games such as **taekkyeon** and the Silla-era *hwarang* warrior tradition are often cited as cultural roots. |\n| 1910–1945 | Under Japanese rule, Korean martial practice was suppressed; many Korean founders learned **Japanese karate** and other arts abroad. |\n| 1944–1950s | After liberation, the first **kwans** (schools) opened in Seoul — Chung Do Kwan, Moo Duk Kwan, Song Moo Kwan, Ji Do Kwan, Chang Moo Kwan and others. |\n| 1955 | The name **Taekwondo** is adopted (tae = foot/kick, kwon = fist, do = way). General **Choi Hong-hi** is a central figure. |\n| 1959–1961 | Korea Taekwondo Association formed to unify the kwans. |\n| 1966 | Choi founds the **ITF** (its patterns: Chon-Ji, Dan-Gun…). |\n| 1972–1973 | **Kukkiwon** (world HQ, Seoul) opens; the **World Taekwondo Federation** is founded (renamed **World Taekwondo** in 2017). |\n| 1988 / 1992 | Demonstration sport at the Seoul and Barcelona Olympics. |\n| 2000 → today | Full Olympic sport; electronic scoring protectors (PSS) and instant video replay introduced. |\n\nTaekwondo today has two big families: **WT/Kukkiwon** (Olympic sparring, Taegeuk poomsae) and **ITF** (semi-contact, Tul patterns, \"sine-wave\" motion). This guide follows **Kukkiwon/WT**.\n\n## 2. Philosophy & etiquette\n\nThe five tenets taught in most schools: **Courtesy (ye-ui), Integrity (yeom-chi), Perseverance (in-nae), Self-control (geuk-gi), Indomitable spirit (baekjul-boolgool).**\n\n- Bow (*kyungye*) when entering/leaving the dojang, to the instructor and to partners.\n- Commands: *charyeot* (attention), *kyungye* (bow), *junbi* (ready), *sijak* (begin), *geuman* (stop), *baro* (return), *swieo* (rest).\n- *Kihap* — a sharp shout from the abdomen on the final/power technique.\n\n## 3. Uniform & modest dressing (no aurat shown)\n\n| Item | Detail | Modest option |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Dobok** | White V-neck top (black V-collar for dan holders; red/black for poom), white trousers. | Long-sleeve, loose-cut dobok; wear a fitted long-sleeve undershirt. |\n| **Ti (belt)** | Rank colour, tied with a square knot at the front. | — |\n| Head | — | Sports hijab / fitted cap (WT allows head covering under the headgear). |\n| Sparring kit | Hogu (trunk protector, red *hong* / blue *chung*), headgear, forearm & shin guards, gloves, groin guard, mouthguard, sensor socks. | Long leggings under the dobok trousers; all protectors go over clothing. |\n\nIn the 3D scene every figure wears a full dobok with long sleeves and trousers, covered feet; heads are smooth skin-tone clay-style heads with black hair and **no facial features**.\n\n## 4. Ranks (levels)\n\n| Level | Belt | Typical time | Required poomsae (Kukkiwon) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 10th–9th gup | White → yellow-stripe | 2–3 mo | Basics, Taegeuk Il Jang |\n| 8th–7th gup | Yellow | 3 mo each | Taegeuk Ee / Sam Jang |\n| 6th–5th gup | Green | 3 mo each | Taegeuk Sa / Oh Jang |\n| 4th–3rd gup | Blue | 3–4 mo each | Taegeuk Yuk / Chil Jang |\n| 2nd–1st gup | Red (red-black) | 4–6 mo each | Taegeuk Pal Jang |\n| 1st dan / poom | Black (poom = under 15) | ~2.5–4 yrs total | Koryo |\n| 2nd–9th dan | Black | years | Keumgang, Taebaek, Pyongwon, Sipjin, Jitae, Cheonkwon, Hansu, Ilyeo |\n\nColours and stripe systems vary between schools; dan certification is issued by **Kukkiwon**.\n\n## 5. Stances (seogi)\n\n| Stance | Weight | Key points |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Ap-seogi** (walking) | 50/50 | One natural step length, both legs almost straight. |\n| **Ap-kubi** (front) | ~2/3 front | Long and wide; front shin vertical, rear leg locked straight, hips square. |\n| **Dwit-kubi** (back) | ~70% rear | Feet form an L; rear knee bent over toes; side-on. |\n| **Juchum-seogi** (horse-riding) | 50/50 | Two foot-lengths wide, knees pushed out, back upright. |\n| **Gyeorugi** (fighting) | 50/50, on balls of feet | Side-on, bouncing, lead hand guarding the face/trunk. |\n\n## 6. Core techniques\n\n**Blocks (makki):** arae makki (low), eolgul makki (high), momtong an makki (inner middle), momtong bakkat makki (outer middle), sonnal makki (knife-hand).\n\n**Strikes:** momtong jireugi (middle punch), dubeon jireugi (double punch), sonnal mok chigi (knife-hand neck strike), deung-joomeok (back fist), palkup (elbow).\n\n**Kicks (chagi):**\n\n| Kick | Target / surface | Coaching cue |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Ap chagi** | Ball of foot to trunk/chin | Chamber knee high, snap, re-chamber. |\n| **Dollyeo chagi** | Instep to trunk/head | Pivot support foot until heel faces target. |\n| **Yeop chagi** | Heel / foot-blade | Hip-shoulder-heel in one line. |\n| **Dwi chagi** | Heel, straight back | Look over the shoulder first, kick second. |\n| **Naeryeo chagi** | Heel downward | Straight leg up, drop like an axe. |\n| **Bandal / Huryeo / Dwi huryeo / Mom dollyeo** | Crescent, hook, spinning hook, 360° kicks | Advanced — learn after the basics are clean. |\n\n## 7. Poomsae (forms)\n\n**Taegeuk Il Jang** (heaven/light, *Keon*) — 18 moves along a 王-shaped line: walking stances, low blocks, middle punches, a front kick sequence and inner blocks. The 3D demo animates moves 1–6 step-by-step. Practise each poomsae for: correct line (embusen), stance length, power at the end of each technique, rhythm, gaze, and kihap.\n\n## 8. Sparring (gyeorugi) & scoring\n\n- **Sebon / Hanbon matsogi** (3-step / 1-step sparring): pre-arranged, for distance and timing (animated in the demo).\n- **Olympic-style WT sparring** (rules change often — always check the current WT rulebook): points from the electronic hogu & headgear — punch to trunk 1, kick to trunk 2, turning kick to trunk 4, kick to head 3, turning kick to head 5. Penalties (*gam-jeom*) give a point to the opponent. Current rules use a round-by-round (best of three rounds) format.\n\n## 9. Strategy & tactics\n\n1. **Distance is everything** — work at \"kicking range\", one step outside punching range.\n2. **Lead-leg tools** (cut kick, fast front-leg roundhouse) to score and to break the opponent's rhythm.\n3. **Counter-fighting** — let the attacker commit, step back or sideways, answer with back kick or roundhouse (demo lesson 15).\n4. **Feints** — shoulder, knee lift, step without kicking — then attack the reaction.\n5. **Corner control** — cut off the ring; avoid stepping out (penalty).\n6. **Combinations** — e.g. cut kick → double roundhouse → spinning back kick.\n7. **Clinch exits** — break and kick immediately; clinching is not scored.\n\n## 10. Strengths & limitations\n\n| Strengths | Limitations |\n|---|---|\n| Fast, long-range kicks, excellent flexibility & footwork | Little grappling / ground work in standard curriculum |\n| Clear rank structure, great for children & discipline | Sport rules reward light, fast scoring kicks over power |\n| Olympic pathway, huge global network | Hands-down habits from sport sparring need correcting for self-defence |\n\n## 11. Training program (beginner, 12 weeks, 3×/week, ~75 min)\n\n| Block | Minutes | Content |\n|---|---|---|\n| Warm-up | 10 | Skipping, joint circles, light jog, dynamic leg swings |\n| Flexibility | 10 | Hamstrings, hip flexors, adductors (split progression) |\n| Basics | 20 | Stances, blocks, punches in lines; 10 reps each side |\n| Kicks | 15 | Chamber drills holding the wall → pads → targets |\n| Poomsae | 10 | One form, 3–5 slow + 3 at speed |\n| Partner | 10 | 3-step sparring → light controlled sparring (week 6+) |\n| Cool-down | 5 | Static stretch, breathing, bow out |\n\n**Progression:** Weeks 1–4 stances/blocks/front & roundhouse; 5–8 side & back kick, Taegeuk 1–2; 9–12 combinations, light sparring, grading prep.\n**Conditioning:** 3×/week — squats, lunges, single-leg balance holds (30 s), core (plank/side plank), plyometric skips, 6×30 s pad-kick intervals.\n\n## 12. Tips & common mistakes\n\n- Pivot the supporting foot on every turning kick — a stuck foot hurts the knee.\n- Chamber high: the knee aims, the lower leg only finishes.\n- Keep the guard up while kicking; the non-kicking arm protects the face.\n- Exhale (or kihap) on impact; don't hold your breath.\n- Stretch **after** warming up, never cold.\n- Film yourself and compare with the 3D side view (Side button) for stance length and posture.\n\n## 13. Safety\n\nMouthguard, groin guard and shin/forearm guards for any contact; controlled contact with beginners; no head kicks for children unless rules allow; stop if dizzy (head impact) and see a doctor.\n\n\n## 15. Glossary\n\n| Korean | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Dojang / Dobok / Ti | Training hall / uniform / belt |\n| Sabeom-nim | Instructor |\n| Seogi / Makki / Jireugi / Chagi | Stance / block / punch / kick |\n| Arae / Momtong / Eolgul | Low / middle / high section |\n| Poomsae / Gyeorugi / Gyeokpa | Forms / sparring / breaking |\n| Hana, dul, set, net, daseot, yeoseot, ilgop, yeodeol, ahop, yeol | 1–10 |\n", "02-silat-gayong": "# 02 · Silat Seni Gayong (Silat Gayung) — Malaysia\n\n> **Origin:** Malay world; formalised in Perak/Singapore in the 1940s · **Type:** complete system — strikes, locks (*kuncian*), throws, weapons · **Spirit:** *\"Gayong\"* evokes inner strength/courage\n\n---\n\n## 1. History & background\n\n- **Roots:** Malay silat developed over centuries in the courts and villages of the archipelago, carried by *pendekar* and *panglima* (warrior-guardians) and taught within Islamic and adat (custom) values. Gayong traces its lineage to Bugis and Malay warrior ancestors.\n- **Mahaguru Dato' Meor Abdul Rahman bin Uda Mohd Hashim (1915–1991)** from Taiping, Perak, systematised and opened Gayong to the public. In **1942**, during the Japanese occupation, he famously taught at **Pulau Sudong (Singapore)**, preparing local youths to defend their community.\n- After the war Gayong spread through the Malay Regiment, police, schools and universities, and was registered as a national body (Pertubuhan Seni Silat Seni Gayong Malaysia). It is one of the largest silat organisations in Malaysia, with branches in Singapore, Brunei, the UK, the USA and elsewhere.\n- **\"Silat Gayung\"** is also used for related but separate lineages — notably **Seni Gayung Fatani** (Pertubuhan Seni Gayung Fatani Malaysia), with Patani roots. Their syllabi differ; this guide follows the mainstream **Seni Gayong** training model in general terms.\n- Malaysia's silat tradition was inscribed on **UNESCO's Intangible Cultural Heritage list in 2019**.\n\n> Syllabus names, belt orders and ceremonies differ between Gayong associations and gelanggang. Always follow your **Jurulatih (instructor)** and **Cikgu/Guru Utama**.\n\n## 2. Adab (etiquette) & values\n\n- Begin with **salam hormat** and doa; respect the gelanggang (training ground), teachers and seniors.\n- Silat is taught as part of character: *budi pekerti*, humility, protecting the weak, never boasting or misusing knowledge.\n- Classes close with salam and advice (*nasihat*). Many gelanggang open with Islamic prayers; members are expected to maintain good akhlak.\n\n## 3. Uniform & modest dressing\n\n| Item | Description |\n|---|---|\n| **Baju silat** | Loose black long-sleeve top (baju Melayu cut) |\n| **Seluar** | Loose black long trousers |\n| **Samping** | Songket/kain cloth wrapped from waist to knee — adds modesty |\n| **Bengkung** | Sash/belt showing rank |\n| **Tengkolok / tanjak** | Folded head cloth (men); women wear **tudung** and the same loose uniform |\n\nEverything is loose, covering arms, legs and body shape — fully compatible with covering aurat. The 3D pesilat wear black baju, songket samping, bengkung and gold tengkolok; faces are blank.\n\n## 4. Levels (bengkung)\n\nA commonly cited Gayong progression (check your association):\n\n| Stage | Bengkung | Focus |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Hitam** (black) — beginner | Adab, kuda-kuda, langkah, basic strikes, elak, simple kuncian |\n| 2 | **Putih** (white) | More buah (applications), falls, releases |\n| 3 | **Hijau** (green) | Advanced locks & takedowns, introduction to weapons |\n| 4 | **Merah** (red) | Weapons (keris, tongkat, pedang, kerambit), multiple attackers |\n| 5 | **Kuning** (yellow) & higher grades | Teacher-level knowledge, ceremonies and responsibilities |\n\nGradings (*ujian*) assess technique, stamina, character and attendance. Advanced lineages keep traditional ceremonies (e.g. *mandi minyak*); these are performed **only** under senior masters with strict safety and religious guidance — never attempt them on your own.\n\n## 5. Kuda-kuda (stances) & langkah (footwork)\n\n| Stance | Use |\n|---|---|\n| **Kuda-kuda hadapan** | Front stance for advancing strikes |\n| **Kuda-kuda tengah** | Horse stance — rooting, receiving, bunga |\n| **Kuda-kuda sisi** | Side stance — presenting a narrow target |\n| **Kuda-kuda belakang** | Rear-weighted — waiting/counter stance, front leg free to kick |\n| **Kuda-kuda rendah** | Low stance — ducking, sweeps, takedowns |\n| **Pasang** | Guard posture: open hands on the centre line, ready to receive |\n\n**Langkah:** triangle (*langkah tiga*) and square (*langkah empat*) patterns teach moving **off** the line of attack, then re-entering at an angle.\n\n## 6. Core techniques\n\n| Category | Examples |\n|---|---|\n| **Bunga / tari** | Flowing hand movements; warm-up, balance, concealment of intent |\n| **Buah pukul** (strikes) | Tumbuk (punch), tetak (hand-edge chop), siku (elbow), lutut (knee), sepak (shin/instep kick), terajang (thrusting sole kick), tampar (palm) |\n| **Elak** (evasion) | Elak sisi, elak bawah, elak belakang |\n| **Tangkis** (parry) | Forearm/palm deflections |\n| **Tangkapan & kuncian** | Catching and joint locks — wrist, elbow, shoulder |\n| **Jatuhan / patahan** | Takedowns; breaking angles (trained with control) |\n| **Senjata** | Keris, tongkat (staff), pedang/parang, kerambit, lembing, cindai (sash) — by level |\n\n## 7. Partner practice in the demo\n\n1. **Elak → tangkis → tangkap → kunci → jatuh** — vs. a straight punch; finishes with a face-down arm lock. *Tap = release.*\n2. **Seni keris: tikam → rampas** — off the line, two-hand check, wrist turned, keris stripped (training keris only).\n3. **Dua penyerang** (3 people) — slip attacker 1, siku, pivot and terajang attacker 2.\n\n## 8. Strategy & tactics\n\n- **Elak dahulu, balas kemudian** — evade first, then counter; never meet force head-on.\n- Attack the **outside** of the opponent's arm so their other hand cannot reach you.\n- Combine **strike → lock → takedown** in one flow; the strike sets up the lock.\n- Against several attackers keep moving, use one attacker as a shield, and never stand between them.\n- Read distance with the bunga: it hides your weight shifts and timing.\n\n## 9. Strengths & limitations\n\n| Strengths | Limitations |\n|---|---|\n| Complete: strikes, locks, throws, weapons, multiple attackers | Syllabus is long; competence takes years |\n| Strong adab and cultural/religious grounding | Less sport-sparring exposure (unless training Silat Olahraga) |\n| Practical angles and evasion | Lineage differences can confuse newcomers |\n\n## 10. Training plan (beginner, 12 weeks)\n\n| Weekly session (2×, ~2 h) | Content |\n|---|---|\n| Opening | Salam, doa, warm-up run & joint mobility (15 min) |\n| Kuda-kuda & langkah | Hold each stance 30–60 s ×3; triangle/square footwork 10 min |\n| Bunga | 10 min slow, breathing through the nose |\n| Buah pukul | Strikes in the air, then on pads — 3×10 each |\n| Elak & tangkis | Partner feeds slow punches; 50 reps each side |\n| Buah (applications) | One new lock/takedown per week, 20 reps slow |\n| Conditioning | Push-ups, squats, sit-ups, neck & grip work |\n| Closing | Nasihat, salam |\n\nWeeks 1–4: adab, stances, bunga, punches/kicks. Weeks 5–8: evasions, parries, first locks, falling (*jatuh selamat*). Weeks 9–12: chained applications, light free play, grading prep.\n\n## 11. Tips & common mistakes\n\n- Keep the back straight in low stances; push the knees out over the toes.\n- In locks, move your **body** around the joint rather than cranking with the arms.\n- Practise falls (*tumbang*) on mats until they are automatic.\n- Slow is smooth, smooth is fast — partner drills start at 30% speed.\n\n## 12. Safety\n\nMats for takedowns; training weapons only (wooden/blunt); release locks immediately on a tap; no full-speed strikes to the head without protection.\n\n\n## 14. Glossary\n\n| Malay | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Gelanggang | Training arena |\n| Jurulatih / Guru | Instructor / master |\n| Pesilat | Practitioner |\n| Kuda-kuda / Langkah | Stance / footwork |\n| Bunga / Tari | Flourish / dance |\n| Buah | Technique/application (\"fruit\") |\n| Elak / Tangkis / Tangkap / Kunci / Jatuh | Evade / parry / catch / lock / fall-takedown |\n| Tumbuk / Tetak / Siku / Lutut / Sepak / Terajang | Punch / chop / elbow / knee / swing kick / thrust kick |\n", "03-silat-cekak": "# 03 · Seni Silat Cekak — Malaysia\n\n> **Origin:** Kedah, Malaysia · **Type:** defensive, application-based silat — **no bunga, no kuda-kuda** · **Signature:** defend and counter in one movement\n\n---\n\n## 1. History & background\n\n- **\"Cekak\"** means *to grip/clasp* (as in gripping the wrist), reflecting the art's reliance on catching and controlling the attacking limb.\n- Tradition traces Cekak to the **Kedah royal court**, attributed to the palace warrior **Panglima Ismail** in the 19th century, and later handed down through a small line of teachers including **Yahya Said**.\n- **Ustaz Hanafi Haji Ahmad** opened the art to the public and founded the national association (**Persatuan Seni Silat Cekak Malaysia**) in the mid-1960s, growing it into one of the most widely practised silat in Malaysian schools and universities.\n- After Ustaz Hanafi's passing, the movement divided into associations including **Seni Silat Cekak Malaysia** and **Seni Silat Cekak Pusaka Ustaz Hanafi** — they share the core principles while differing in organisation and details.\n- Cekak is taught with strong Islamic values and adab; many associations accept students through an *akuan* (pledge) and close their syllabus with an *ijazah* (graduation) ceremony.\n\n> Details of lineage, syllabus naming and ceremonies differ between Cekak associations — always follow your *jurulatih*.\n\n## 2. Principles that make Cekak different\n\n| Principle | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Tiada bunga** | No ornamental dance — training time goes into applications. |\n| **Tiada kuda-kuda** | No fixed stance: you defend from how you naturally stand/walk. |\n| **Tidak mengelak** | You don't dodge away; you **meet** the attack, usually entering at an angle. |\n| **Tangkis sambil serang** | Parry and strike **at the same moment** — one hand meets, the other hits. |\n| **Tidak menyerang dahulu** | Cekak is defensive: it responds to an attack, it does not start the fight. |\n| **Kunci & jatuh** | Follow the strike with a lock and take the attacker down under control. |\n\n## 3. Uniform & modest dressing\n\nPlain **black baju silat** (long-sleeve) and **loose black seluar**, often with **samping** and a **bengkung**; men may wear a black tengkolok, women wear **tudung** with the same loose uniform. It is naturally modest (aurat covered, body shape hidden). The 3D figures wear black, songket samping, gold bengkung and a covered head; no faces.\n\n## 4. Levels\n\nCekak is usually taught as a series of **kelas** (course stages) rather than many belts. A commonly described progression is:\n\n| Stage | Focus |\n|---|---|\n| **Asas** (foundation) | Adab, principles, the core set of buah against punches, grabs and kicks |\n| **Jatuh** | Takedown-oriented buah, falls |\n| **Potong** | \"Cutting\" counters — intercepting the attack before it lands |\n| **Umum / Khas** | Combined and advanced applications, weapon defences (pisau, parang, kayu) |\n| **Ijazah / Jurulatih** | Graduation, teaching qualification |\n\n## 5. Technique families (buah)\n\n| Attack | Typical Cekak answer |\n|---|---|\n| Straight punch (*tumbukan*) | Meet the forearm with one hand + strike ribs with the other → cekak wrist → lock → take down |\n| Wrist grab (*pegang*) | Rotate out through the thumb gap + simultaneous strike → step through |\n| Front kick (*sepak*) | Enter at an angle, scoop the leg, push the chest, sweep the base |\n| Knife thrust (*tikam pisau*) | Two hands on the arm off the line → turn the wrist against the elbow → disarm |\n| Rear bear-hug | Drop the weight, elbow back, turn and push out |\n| Collar/hair grab, headlock | Pin the gripping hand, strike, lock |\n\n## 6. Strategy & tactics\n\n1. **One beat, not two** — defence and counter overlap; it denies the attacker a second attack.\n2. **Enter at an angle** — move toward the outside of the attacking arm.\n3. **Control the limb** (cekak) — once the wrist is held, the attacker's balance follows the lock.\n4. **Finish on the ground** — attacker down, you standing or kneeling with control.\n5. **Stay natural** — because there's no stance, your defence works from walking, sitting or standing.\n\n## 7. Strengths & limitations\n\n| Strengths | Limitations |\n|---|---|\n| Fast to learn practical self-defence | Little sport sparring/competition experience |\n| Works from natural posture | Pre-arranged drills need pressure testing to stay realistic |\n| Strong values, community and Islamic adab | Admission and ceremonies vary by association |\n\n## 8. Training plan (12 weeks, 2 sessions/week)\n\n| Segment | Minutes | Content |\n|---|---|---|\n| Opening | 10 | Salam, doa, mobility |\n| Principles | 10 | Tangkis-sambil-serang drill on both sides, 3×20 |\n| New buah | 30 | Instructor demo → slow pairs → normal speed (attacker 50% → 80%) |\n| Review | 20 | All previous buah, rotate partners |\n| Falls | 10 | Front/back/side breakfalls on mats |\n| Conditioning | 15 | Grip strength, push-ups, squats, core |\n| Closing | 5 | Nasihat, salam |\n\n## 9. Tips\n\n- Don't pull back when grabbed — rotate toward the thumb and move your body.\n- The striking hand is not a \"decoration\": hit the ribs/face with intent (controlled in training).\n- Train as both attacker and defender; a good attacker (*penyerang*) makes good defenders.\n- Keep weapons drills with blunt trainers only.\n\n\n## 11. Glossary\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Cekak | Grip / clasp |\n| Buah | Technique |\n| Penyerang / Pesilat | Attacker / defender |\n| Tangkis sambil serang | Parry while striking |\n| Kunci / Jatuh | Lock / takedown |\n| Ijazah | Graduation |\n", "04-pencak-silat": "# 04 · Pencak Silat — Indonesia & the Nusantara\n\n> **Origin:** Indonesian archipelago (with sister traditions in Malaysia, Brunei, Singapore, southern Thailand, the Philippines) · **Type:** striking, sweeps, catches, takedowns, weapons, artistic forms\n> **Bodies:** IPSI (Indonesia, est. 1948), **PERSILAT** (international, est. 1980) · **UNESCO:** \"Traditions of Pencak Silat\" inscribed 2019\n\n---\n\n## 1. History & background\n\n- *Pencak* (the artistic/performance side) + *silat* (the fighting application). Hundreds of styles (*aliran*) grew from village, court and religious schools — e.g. **Cimande, Cikalong** (West Java), **Setia Hati / PSHT** (East Java), **Merpati Putih, Tapak Suci** (Muhammadiyah), **Perisai Diri, Bakti Negara** (Bali).\n- Silat was taught alongside Islamic learning in *pesantren* and used in resistance against colonial powers.\n- **IPSI** (Ikatan Pencak Silat Indonesia) founded **18 May 1948**; **PERSILAT** founded **1980** in Jakarta to spread the sport worldwide.\n- Competitive pencak silat appears at the **SEA Games** (since 1987) and the **Asian Games** (2018); world championships run under PERSILAT.\n\n## 2. Philosophy & values\n\nPencak silat education rests on four aspects: **mental-spiritual**, **self-defence**, **art (seni)** and **sport (olahraga)**. Respect (*hormat*), brotherhood (*persaudaraan*) and self-control are emphasised; most perguruan begin and end with **salam** and doa.\n\n## 3. Uniform & modest dressing\n\n- Competition uniform: **black, loose, long-sleeve top and long trousers**, with a **sabuk** (belt) — modest by design.\n- Tanding: red (*merah*) or blue (*biru*) corner **body protector** worn over the uniform; groin guard, mouthguard; shin guards in some events.\n- Female athletes compete with **hijab** routinely in Southeast Asia.\n- In the 3D demo all figures wear black uniforms with an open-face hijab covering hair, neck and shoulders, covered feet, and smooth skin-tone faces with no facial features.\n\n## 4. Levels\n\nEach perguruan has its own belt sequence (e.g. polos/white → yellow → green → blue → brown → black → masters). Typical content per stage:\n\n| Stage | Focus |\n|---|---|\n| Dasar (basic) | Salam, kuda-kuda, sikap pasang, pola langkah, punches, kicks, basic blocks |\n| Menengah (intermediate) | Sweeps (sapuan), catches (tangkapan), takedowns (jatuhan), jurus sets |\n| Lanjut (advanced) | Weapons (golok, toya, pisau), ganda/regu, guntingan, coaching |\n| Pendekar / master | Teaching, philosophy, lineage knowledge |\n\n## 5. Basics\n\n| Group | Content |\n|---|---|\n| **Kuda-kuda** | Depan (front), tengah (middle), samping (side), belakang (rear), silang (crossed) |\n| **Sikap pasang** | Guard postures — open-hand and fighting guards |\n| **Pola langkah** | Stepping patterns: straight, triangle (*segitiga*), square, zig-zag |\n| **Belaan** | Tangkisan (parry), elakan (evasion), hindaran (avoidance) |\n| **Serangan** | Pukulan lurus/bandul/sangkol (straight/swing/upper), tendangan depan, sabit (crescent/roundhouse), T (side), belakang |\n| **Jatuhan** | Sapuan (sweep), guntingan (scissors), kaitan (hook), tangkapan + jatuhan |\n\n## 6. Competition categories\n\n| Category | Description |\n|---|---|\n| **Tanding** | Full-contact sparring on the body protector. Points (PERSILAT): punch 1, kick 2, takedown (jatuhan) 3; plus judges' points for successful defence/catch. Head strikes are not allowed. |\n| **Tunggal** | Solo set of 100 movements: empty hand, **golok** (machete) and **toya** (staff), within a fixed time. |\n| **Ganda** | Two athletes performing pre-arranged attack/defence (empty hand and weapons), judged for realism & flow. |\n| **Regu** | Team of three performing the standard set in unison. |\n| **Solo kreatif** | Free-composition solo (in some events). |\n\nRules are revised regularly — check the latest PERSILAT/IPSI rulebook.\n\n## 7. Strategy (tanding)\n\n1. **Kicks score, takedowns win** — a single jatuhan (3) outweighs a kick (2).\n2. **Catch and sweep** — invite the sabit, absorb with the arm, trap, sweep the base leg (demo lesson 4).\n3. **Side kick to stop forward pressure**, then counter.\n4. **Angle off with pola langkah** — triangle steps put you outside the opponent's line.\n5. **Finish each exchange in sikap pasang** — judges reward control and composure.\n\n## 8. Strengths & limitations\n\n| Strengths | Limitations |\n|---|---|\n| Rich mix: striking + takedowns + weapons + art | Huge style diversity — quality varies |\n| Effective low sweeps and catches | Sport rules exclude head strikes |\n| Strong cultural/spiritual identity | Ground fighting less developed than BJJ/judo |\n\n## 9. Training program\n\n| Day | Session |\n|---|---|\n| Mon | Kuda-kuda holds, pola langkah, punch/kick lines (200 reps), core |\n| Wed | Tanding drills: catch-and-sweep pairs, kick-counter, 4×2 min light sparring |\n| Fri | Tunggal/jurus practice, flexibility, weapon basics |\n| Sat | Conditioning: intervals (8×200 m), plyometrics, neck/grip |\n\n## 10. Tips\n\n- Tendangan sabit: turn the supporting foot and hit with the instep/shin.\n- Keep the hands alive during kicks — you must be ready to catch.\n- In tunggal, power and **\"ekspresi\"** (spirit) matter as much as accuracy.\n- Learn breakfalls before practising sweeps and guntingan.\n\n\n## 12. Glossary\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Perguruan / Aliran | School / style |\n| Pesilat / Pendekar | Practitioner / master |\n| Sikap pasang | Guard posture |\n| Pola langkah | Step pattern |\n| Tendangan sabit / T | Crescent-roundhouse kick / side kick |\n| Sapuan / Guntingan / Jatuhan | Sweep / scissors / takedown |\n| Golok / Toya | Machete / staff |\n", "05-karate": "# 05 · Karate-dō (空手道) — \"The Way of the Empty Hand\"\n\n> **Origin:** Okinawa (Ryukyu Kingdom) → Japan · **Type:** striking — punches, kicks, blocks; throws & locks in kata applications (*bunkai*)\n> **Major styles:** Shotokan, Gōjū-ryū, Shitō-ryū, Wadō-ryū (plus Kyokushin full-contact) · **Bodies:** WKF (sport), style federations (e.g. JKA)\n\n---\n\n## 1. History & background\n\n| Period | Event |\n|---|---|\n| 14th–19th c. | Okinawan fighting art *te/tī* mixes with Chinese boxing (*tōde*, \"Tang hand\") through trade with Fujian. Centres: **Shuri, Naha, Tomari**. |\n| Late 1800s | Masters such as **Itosu Ankō** simplify kata (creating the Pinan/Heian series) to teach karate in Okinawan schools (1901+). |\n| 1922 | **Gichin Funakoshi** demonstrates karate in Tokyo; the characters change from \"Chinese hand\" to \"**empty hand**\" (空手). |\n| 1930s | Styles form: Gōjū-ryū (Miyagi Chōjun), Shitō-ryū (Mabuni Kenwa), Wadō-ryū (Ōtsuka Hironori), Shotokan (Funakoshi's students). |\n| 1949 | **Japan Karate Association (JKA)** founded (Shotokan). |\n| 1964 | Kyokushin (Ōyama Masutatsu) — knockdown full contact. |\n| 1970/1990 | WUKO → **World Karate Federation (WKF)**. |\n| 2021 | Olympic debut at the Tokyo 2020 Games (kata and kumite). |\n\n## 2. Philosophy & etiquette\n\n**Dōjō kun:** Seek perfection of character · Be faithful · Endeavour · Respect others · Refrain from violent behaviour.\nFunakoshi's precept: ***Karate ni sente nashi*** — \"there is no first attack in karate.\"\nBow (*rei*) entering the dōjō, to the *sensei*, to partners; *kiai* (spirit shout) on decisive techniques.\n\n## 3. Uniform & modest dressing\n\n**Karategi** — white, heavy cotton jacket (long sleeves), loose trousers, **obi**. For modesty: long-sleeve fitted undershirt, the jacket tied securely, women's/modest athletes may wear a **sports hijab** (WKF permits head covering for female athletes). Kumite: red (*aka*) or blue (*ao*) belt and mitts, mouthguard, body protector, shin & instep pads. The 3D karateka wear a full gi and covered feet, with smooth skin-tone heads (black hair, no facial features).\n\n## 4. Ranks (kyū / dan) — Shotokan example\n\n| Kyū | Common belt | Kata |\n|---|---|---|\n| 10–9 | White / yellow | Kihon, Heian Shodan |\n| 8 | Orange | Heian Nidan |\n| 7 | Green | Heian Sandan |\n| 6 | Purple | Heian Yondan |\n| 5–4 | Purple/blue | Heian Godan, Tekki Shodan |\n| 3–1 | Brown | Bassai Dai, Kanku Dai, Jion, Empi… |\n| 1st dan+ | Black | Senior kata (Hangetsu, Jitte, Gankaku…) |\n\nColours vary by club and style.\n\n## 5. Kihon (basics)\n\n**Dachi (stances):** heiko, musubi, zenkutsu (front), kōkutsu (back), kiba (straddle), neko-ashi (cat), sanchin (hourglass), fudō/sochin.\n**Uke (blocks):** gedan-barai, age-uke, soto-uke, uchi-uke, shutō-uke.\n**Tsuki & uchi (punches/strikes):** oi-zuki, gyaku-zuki, kizami-zuki, uraken, shutō-uchi, empi (elbow).\n**Geri (kicks):** mae-geri, mawashi-geri, yoko-geri kekomi/keage, ushiro-geri, ura-mawashi-geri.\n**Key mechanics:** *hikite* (pull hand), hip rotation (*koshi*), *kime* (focus at impact), *zanshin* (lingering awareness).\n\n## 6. Kata\n\nKata are encyclopaedias of technique. Beginners learn **Heian Shodan**: turn left gedan-barai → step oi-zuki → turn 180° gedan-barai → tettsui → oi-zuki → turn to front gedan-barai → three age-uke (kiai on the third) → … (21 moves, \"I\"-shaped embusen). The demo animates the first eight moves.\n\n**Bunkai** = applications of kata movements with a partner (strikes, joint locks, throws).\n\n## 7. Kumite\n\n| Type | Description |\n|---|---|\n| Gohon / Sanbon kumite | 5-/3-step pre-arranged |\n| **Kihon ippon kumite** | One-step: attack announced, block + counter (demo) |\n| Jiyu ippon | Semi-free one-step |\n| **Shiai kumite** (WKF) | Free sparring, controlled contact. **Yuko** (1) punch; **waza-ari** (2) body kick; **ippon** (3) head kick or takedown + finishing punch |\n\n## 8. Strategy & tactics\n\n- **Ma-ai** (distance): stay just outside the opponent's reach; step in only to finish.\n- **Sen no sen / go no sen**: attack the opponent's intention, or counter after their attack.\n- Feint high (kizami), score body (gyaku-zuki) — demo lesson 8.\n- Head kicks score highest; use them when the guard drops to protect the body.\n- Return to kamae and show *zanshin* after every technique — referees look for it.\n\n## 9. Strengths & limitations\n\n| Strengths | Limitations |\n|---|---|\n| Powerful linear strikes, great body mechanics | Point sparring can encourage pulling techniques |\n| Kata build memory, balance, discipline | Limited ground grappling |\n| Huge worldwide availability | Quality varies — choose a club that spars |\n\n## 10. Training plan\n\n| Day | Content |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Kihon lines: 20 reps per technique per side; stance holds |\n| 2 | Kata: 5× slow, 5× full speed; bunkai with partner |\n| 3 | Kumite drills: footwork, kizami–gyaku combos, light sparring 5×2 min |\n| + | Conditioning: makiwara or pad work, push-ups on knuckles (progressive), squats, core |\n\n## 11. Tips\n\n- Power comes from the hips and the floor, not the arm.\n- Snap the pull hand (hikite) as hard as the punch.\n- Keep the heel of the back foot down in zenkutsu and gyaku-zuki.\n- Quality > quantity: 10 perfect reps beat 100 sloppy ones.\n\n\n## 13. Glossary\n\n| Japanese | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Sensei / Senpai / Kōhai | Teacher / senior / junior |\n| Kihon / Kata / Kumite | Basics / forms / sparring |\n| Dachi / Uke / Tsuki / Geri | Stance / block / punch / kick |\n| Jōdan / Chūdan / Gedan | Upper / middle / lower |\n| Hajime / Yame / Yoi / Rei | Begin / stop / ready / bow |\n| Ichi, ni, san, shi, go, roku, shichi, hachi, ku, jū | 1–10 |\n", "06-judo": "# 06 · Judo (柔道) — \"The Gentle Way\"\n\n> **Origin:** Japan, 1882 · **Founder:** Kanō Jigorō · **Type:** throws (*nage-waza*), pins, chokes and armlocks (*katame-waza*)\n> **Bodies:** Kodokan (Tokyo), International Judo Federation (IJF) · **Olympic:** since 1964 (women since 1992)\n\n---\n\n## 1. History & background\n\n- **Kanō Jigorō (1860–1938)**, a small, scholarly man, studied the classical jūjutsu schools Tenjin Shin'yō-ryū and Kitō-ryū, removed the dangerous techniques, and organised what remained around scientific principles. In **1882** he opened the **Kōdōkan** in Tokyo with nine students.\n- Judo beat rival jūjutsu schools in the Tokyo police tournaments of the 1880s and became part of the Japanese school system.\n- Kanō became the first Asian member of the IOC (1909). Judo debuted at the **Tokyo 1964 Olympics**; the IJF (founded 1951) governs international competition.\n- Judo strongly influenced **Brazilian Jiu-Jitsu**, sambo and modern self-defence.\n\n## 2. Principles & etiquette\n\n- **Seiryoku zen'yō** — maximum efficiency with minimum effort.\n- **Jita kyōei** — mutual welfare and benefit: you improve *with* your partner, not at their expense.\n- **Jū** — yielding: use the opponent's force against them.\n- **Rei** (bow) on and off the tatami, to sensei and to partner — standing (*ritsurei*) or kneeling (*zarei*).\n- Tori = the one who throws; Uke = the one who receives (and falls).\n\n## 3. Uniform & modest dressing\n\n**Judogi:** thick double-weave jacket (long sleeves to the wrist), long trousers, **obi**. Competition: one athlete in white, one in blue. For modesty: a fitted long-sleeve rash guard and leggings under the gi; sports head coverings are accepted at many national/regional events — always check the current dress rules of your federation. In the demo: white and blue judogi; tori has black hair, uke wears an open-face hijab; smooth skin-tone faces with no features.\n\n## 4. Ranks\n\n| Grade | Typical belt | Content |\n|---|---|---|\n| 6th–5th kyū | White → yellow | Ukemi, posture, grips, first throws (o-goshi, o-soto-gari, de-ashi-barai), kesa-gatame |\n| 4th–3rd kyū | Orange → green | Seoi-nage, uchi-mata, harai-goshi, osaekomi escapes, first shime-waza |\n| 2nd–1st kyū | Blue → brown | Combinations, counters, kansetsu-waza, kata basics |\n| 1st–5th dan | Black | Competition/technical knowledge, Nage-no-kata, Katame-no-kata, teaching |\n| 6th–8th dan | Red-white | Senior masters |\n| 9th–10th dan | Red | Very rare honours |\n\nBelt colours vary by country.\n\n## 5. Ukemi (breakfalls) — learn first!\n\n| Ukemi | Key point |\n|---|---|\n| **Ushiro ukemi** | Back fall — chin tucked, round back, both arms slap at ~45° |\n| **Yoko ukemi** | Side fall — one arm slaps, legs apart, head up |\n| **Mae ukemi** | Forward fall onto forearms & palms |\n| **Mae mawari ukemi** | Rolling fall — shoulder to opposite hip diagonal |\n\n## 6. Throwing: kuzushi → tsukuri → kake\n\n1. **Kuzushi** — break uke's balance (8 directions: front, back, sides, corners).\n2. **Tsukuri** — fit in: place your body in the position to throw.\n3. **Kake** — execute the throw.\n\n**Gokyo no waza** (the classic 40 throws, Kōdōkan 1920) groups throws into hand (*te*), hip (*koshi*), foot/leg (*ashi*) and sacrifice (*sutemi*) techniques. Starting set:\n\n| Throw | Group | Essence |\n|---|---|---|\n| **O-goshi** | Koshi | Hip below uke's, arm around waist, lift & rotate |\n| **Seoi-nage** | Te | Drop under, back to uke's chest, bow forward |\n| **O-soto-gari** | Ashi | Uke on the heel, reap behind the thigh |\n| **De-ashi-barai** | Ashi | Sweep the advancing foot before weight lands |\n| Uchi-mata, harai-goshi, tai-otoshi, ko-uchi-gari, tomoe-nage | various | Next steps |\n\n## 7. Katame-waza (ground)\n\n- **Osaekomi-waza (pins):** kesa-gatame, kata-gatame, yoko-shiho-gatame, kami-shiho-gatame, tate-shiho-gatame.\n- **Shime-waza (strangles):** nami-juji-jime, okuri-eri-jime, hadaka-jime (juniors/adults only, by rule).\n- **Kansetsu-waza (armlocks):** ude-hishigi-juji-gatame (armbar), ude-garami. Only elbow locks are allowed.\n\n## 8. Competition (IJF — check current rules)\n\n- Contest: 4 minutes, **golden score** if tied.\n- **Ippon** (wins): throw largely on the back with force/speed/control; 20 s pin; submission. **Waza-ari**: lesser throw or 10 s pin; **two waza-ari = ippon**.\n- **Shido** penalties (e.g. passivity, illegal grips); third shido = loss (*hansoku-make*). Grabbing the legs directly is penalised.\n\n## 9. Strategy & tactics\n\n1. **Win the grip (kumikata)** — the athlete who controls the sleeve/lapel controls the fight.\n2. **Action–reaction** — push to make uke push back, then throw forward (and vice versa).\n3. **Combinations (renraku-waza)** — ko-uchi-gari → seoi-nage; o-uchi-gari → uchi-mata.\n4. **Counters (kaeshi-waza)** — ura-nage, tsubame-gaeshi.\n5. **Transition to ne-waza** — after a partial throw, go straight to a pin or armlock.\n\n## 10. Strengths & limitations\n\n| Strengths | Limitations |\n|---|---|\n| Real resistance training (randori) every class | No striking |\n| Superb balance, throws and pins | Competition rules limit leg grabs and many submissions |\n| Safe-falling skills for life | Hard on the body without good ukemi |\n\n## 11. Training plan\n\n| Part | Minutes | Content |\n|---|---|---|\n| Warm-up | 15 | Running, shrimping, forward/backward rolls |\n| Ukemi | 10 | All four types; partner-assisted falls |\n| Uchikomi | 15 | 3×10 entries per throw (no throw), then 10 *nagekomi* (with throw) |\n| Ne-waza | 15 | Pins, escapes, turnovers; 4×2 min ne-waza randori |\n| Tachi-waza randori | 15 | 5×3 min |\n| Conditioning | 10 | Pull-ups, rope climbs, grip work, sprints |\n\n## 12. Tips\n\n- Tuck the chin on every fall — always.\n- Bend your knees to get **under** uke's centre of gravity for hip throws.\n- Don't stiff-arm; keep elbows down and relaxed to feel uke's balance.\n- Learn throws on both sides early.\n\n\n## 14. Glossary\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Tori / Uke | Thrower / receiver |\n| Hajime / Matte / Sore-made | Begin / wait / end |\n| Osaekomi / Toketa | Pin on / pin broken |\n| Uchikomi / Nagekomi / Randori | Entry reps / throwing reps / free practice |\n| Kumikata / Kuzushi | Gripping / balance breaking |\n", "07-wing-chun": "# 07 · Wing Chun (詠春) — Southern Chinese Kung Fu\n\n> **Origin:** Southern China (Guangdong) · **Type:** close-range striking & trapping on the centre line · **Famous lineage:** Yip Man (Ip Man) of Foshan → Hong Kong\n\n---\n\n## 1. History & background\n\n- **Legend:** the Buddhist nun **Ng Mui**, one of the \"Five Elders\" who escaped the destruction of a Shaolin temple, created a fast, economical system and taught it to **Yim Wing-chun**, who used it to fend off an unwanted suitor. (Treat this as tradition; documented history begins later.)\n- **19th century:** the art is associated with the **Red Boat Opera** troupes of Guangdong; **Leung Jan**, a Foshan herbalist, became a renowned master; his student **Chan Wah-shun** taught **Yip Man**.\n- **Yip Man (1893–1972)** moved to **Hong Kong in 1949** and taught publicly from 1950; students included **Bruce Lee**, Wong Shun-leung, William Cheung, Chu Shong-tin and many others, spreading Wing Chun worldwide.\n- Many lineages exist (Yip Man, Pan Nam, Gu Lao, Pao Fa Lien, Hung Fa Yi…) with different spellings (Wing Tsun, Ving Tsun).\n\n## 2. Principles\n\n| Principle | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Centre-line theory** | Attack and guard the vertical line through the body's vital points |\n| **Economy of motion** | Shortest path: straight punches, no wind-ups |\n| **Simultaneous attack & defence** (*lin siu dai da*) | Block and strike in the same beat |\n| **Facing** | Square up to the opponent so both hands can work |\n| **Sensitivity** | Feel pressure through contact (chi sau) instead of guessing |\n| **Kuen kuit** (maxims) | e.g. \"*Lat sau jik chung*\" — when the hand is freed, thrust forward |\n\n## 3. Uniform & modest dressing\n\nTraditional **black kung-fu jacket** (Chinese collar, frog buttons, long sleeves) and **loose trousers**, cloth shoes, sometimes a sash. Many schools use T-shirts — for modesty choose the long-sleeve jacket or long-sleeve top and loose trousers, and a head covering if desired. In the demo: black jacket with sash, black hair, smooth featureless skin-tone face; the senior (si-hing) wears red.\n\n## 4. Levels\n\nThere is no universal belt system; progress is measured by forms and skills. Common sequence:\n\n| Stage | Forms & skills |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | **Siu Nim Tau** (\"little idea\") — stance, structure, basic hand shapes, chain punch; *dan chi sau* (single sticking hands) |\n| 2 | **Chum Kiu** (\"seeking the bridge\") — turning (juen ma), stepping, kicks; *poon sau* (double rolling hands) |\n| 3 | **Biu Jee** (\"thrusting fingers\") — emergency techniques, recovering the centre, elbows |\n| 4 | **Muk Yan Jong** (wooden dummy, ~108/116 movements in the Yip Man line) |\n| 5 | **Luk Dim Bun Kwan** (long pole) & **Baat Jaam Dao** (butterfly knives) |\n\nSome organisations add student/technician/practitioner grades.\n\n## 5. Core techniques\n\n| Technique | Description |\n|---|---|\n| Yee Jee Kim Yeung Ma | Stance: knees in, toes in, pelvis tucked |\n| Man sau / Wu sau | Asking hand (front) / guarding hand (rear) |\n| **Tan sau** | Palm-up dispersing arm |\n| **Bong sau** | Wing arm: elbow higher than wrist; used under pressure, never held |\n| **Fook sau** | Bridge-on, bent-wrist controlling arm |\n| **Pak sau** | Slapping palm |\n| **Lap sau** | Pulling hand |\n| Jum / Gan / Huen sau | Sinking elbow / splitting arm / circling hand |\n| **Yat jee chung kuen** | Vertical \"sun\" fist; **lin wan kuen** = chain punches |\n| Low kicks | Front heel kick, side heel kick, stamp to knee/shin |\n\n## 6. Strategy\n\n- Close the distance **behind** a straight punch; stay glued once in contact.\n- Occupy the centre — the opponent must go around you, which is slower.\n- Use **trapping** (pak, lap) to tie up arms for one beat, then hit.\n- Kicks low (knee, shin) while the hands control the upper body.\n- \"Don't chase hands\": strike the body/face, not the opponent's arms.\n\n## 7. Strengths & limitations\n\n| Strengths | Limitations |\n|---|---|\n| Very efficient close-range striking | Less long-range kicking & ground game |\n| Develops excellent tactile sensitivity | Chi sau skills must be pressure-tested with free sparring |\n| Suitable for smaller people; little flexibility needed | Lineage politics, variable quality |\n\n## 8. Training plan\n\n| Daily (30–45 min) | Weekly (class 2×) |\n|---|---|\n| Siu Nim Tau ×2 slow (10 min) | Chi sau rotations with several partners |\n| Chain punches 3×1 min on a wall bag | Drills: pak-da, lap-da, bong-lap |\n| Stance holds 3×2 min | Dummy work (when learnt) |\n| Forearm/wrist conditioning | Light sparring with gloves |\n\n## 9. Tips\n\n- Relax the shoulders — tension kills speed and sensitivity.\n- Keep the elbow of the tan/fook sau one fist from the body, on the centre line.\n- Chain punches come from the elbow; drop the shoulders and keep the wrist straight.\n- In chi sau, feel — don't force.\n\n\n## 11. Glossary\n\n| Cantonese | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Sifu / Si-hing / Si-dai | Teacher / senior / junior classmate |\n| Kwoon | Training hall |\n| Sau / Kuen / Geuk | Hand / fist / leg |\n| Chi sau | Sticking hands |\n| Muk yan jong | Wooden dummy |\n", "08-muay-thai": "# 08 · Muay Thai (มวยไทย) — \"The Art of Eight Limbs\"\n\n> **Origin:** Thailand (Siam) · **Type:** stand-up striking with fists, elbows, knees, shins + clinch · **Bodies:** IFMA (amateur, IOC-recognised), WMC & national stadiums (pro)\n\n---\n\n## 1. History & background\n\n- Developed from **Muay Boran** (ancient boxing) and the battlefield art **Krabi Krabong** of the Siamese armies.\n- **Nai Khanom Tom**: captured by the Burmese after the fall of Ayutthaya (1767), he reportedly defeated a series of Burmese fighters in **1774**; **17 March** is celebrated as National Muay Thai Day.\n- Fighters once bound their hands in hemp rope (*kard chuek*); during the **1920s–30s** boxing gloves, rings, rounds and weight classes were introduced.\n- Famous stadiums: **Rajadamnern** (1945) and **Lumpinee** (1956) in Bangkok.\n- Today Muay Thai is a national sport, a global fitness and fight sport, and the striking base of MMA. **IFMA** governs amateur competition and is recognised by the IOC.\n\n## 2. Culture & etiquette\n\n- **Wai Kru Ram Muay** — a pre-fight ritual honouring teachers (*kru*), parents and the art; the fighter circles the ring, kneels, bows and performs a dance, accompanied by **Sarama** music.\n- **Mongkol** (headband) is placed by the kru before the fight and removed before the first round; **prajioud** (armbands) may be worn during the fight.\n- Respect the kru and the ring: step over the top rope when entering, never under.\n\n## 3. Modest fight kit (aurat covered)\n\nTraditional kit is shorts only, but modest practice is common and simple:\n\n| Item | Modest choice |\n|---|---|\n| Top | Long-sleeve **rash guard** (compression top) |\n| Legs | Full-length leggings/spats **under** the Muay Thai shorts |\n| Head | Sports hijab or fitted cap (under headgear in amateur bouts) |\n| Protective | Gloves, hand wraps, shin guards, mouthguard, groin guard, headgear & elbow pads (amateur) |\n\nCheck your federation's rules for competition — many amateur bodies accommodate long sleeves and head coverings. The 3D fighters wear exactly this kit (long sleeves + leggings under red/blue shorts), mongkol & prajioud for the Wai Kru, smooth skin-tone heads with black hair and no facial features.\n\n## 4. Levels\n\nTraditional camps have **no belts** — you progress by skill and fights. Some organisations use a **Khan (or Prajioud) grading system**, e.g.:\n\n| Stage | Focus |\n|---|---|\n| Beginner (Khan 1–3) | Stance, footwork, jab/cross/hook/uppercut, teep, roundhouse, guard |\n| Intermediate (Khan 4–6) | Elbows, knees, checks, combinations, basic clinch |\n| Advanced (Khan 7–10) | Counters, sweeps, clinch control, ring craft, fight experience |\n| Instructor (Khan 11+) | Coaching, pad holding, Wai Kru knowledge |\n\n## 5. The eight limbs\n\n| Weapon | Thai | Key techniques |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fists | **Mat** | Jab (mat trong), cross, hook (mat wiang), uppercut (mat ngat), superman punch |\n| Elbows | **Sok** | Sok tat (horizontal), sok ngat (uppercut), sok tong (downward), sok klap (spinning) |\n| Knees | **Khao** | Khao trong (straight), khao chiang (diagonal), khao loi (flying) |\n| Feet/shins | **Teep / Te** | Teep (push kick), te tat (roundhouse), low kick, switch kick |\n| Clinch | **Chap kho / Plum** | Neck control, off-balancing, knee strikes |\n\n## 6. Scoring (traditional / pro — check event rules)\n\n5 rounds × 3 min, 2-min rests. Judges favour **clean, powerful kicks, knees and elbows**, effective clinch work, balance, and **ring control / composure** (fighters showing they are unhurt). Rounds 1–2 are often lighter \"feeling-out\" rounds; the fight is usually decided in rounds 3–5.\n\n## 7. Strategy & tactics\n\n1. **Teep** to manage distance and score — \"the jab of the legs\".\n2. **Check** every low/mid kick (shin to shin), then counter instantly (demo lesson 8).\n3. Kick **through** the target with the shin — power comes from the hip turn.\n4. **Clinch**: win inside position, break posture, knee the body, off-balance for sweeps.\n5. **Show no damage** — composure and balance influence judges.\n6. Southpaw/orthodox: kick the open side; step outside the lead foot.\n\n## 8. Strengths & limitations\n\n| Strengths | Limitations |\n|---|---|\n| Devastating, proven striking & clinch | No ground grappling |\n| Outstanding conditioning | High injury risk in hard sparring |\n| Simple, battle-tested techniques | Traditional rules allow elbows — needs protective gear in training |\n\n## 9. Training plan (fight-camp style, scaled for beginners)\n\n| Session part | Duration |\n|---|---|\n| Run / skipping | 15–20 min |\n| Shadow boxing | 3 × 3 min |\n| Pads with kru | 4–5 × 3 min |\n| Heavy bag | 3–5 × 3 min (kicks 50 per leg) |\n| Technical sparring / clinch | 3–5 rounds, light |\n| Conditioning | Sit-ups, push-ups, neck work, 100 teeps |\n| Stretch | 10 min |\n\nBeginners: 3×/week; amateur fighters: 5–6×/week with a coach.\n\n## 10. Tips\n\n- Turn the supporting foot onto the ball/toes when kicking; arm swings down for counter-balance.\n- Keep the chin down and the guard high — elbows are sharp.\n- Breathe out on every strike (\"tsss\").\n- Condition shins gradually (bag work, not hitting hard objects).\n\n\n## 12. Glossary\n\n| Thai | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Kru / Nak muay | Teacher / fighter |\n| Wai Kru Ram Muay | Pre-fight homage & dance |\n| Mongkol / Prajioud | Headband / armband |\n| Mat / Sok / Khao / Te / Teep | Punch / elbow / knee / kick / push kick |\n| Chap kho (plum) | Neck clinch |\n", "09-bjj": "# 09 · Brazilian Jiu-Jitsu (BJJ)\n\n> **Origin:** Brazil, from Kōdōkan judo/jūjutsu · **Type:** grappling — positions, sweeps, passes and submissions, especially on the ground\n> **Bodies:** IBJJF (gi & no-gi), ADCC (submission grappling), and others\n\n---\n\n## 1. History & background\n\n- **Mitsuyo Maeda** (\"Conde Koma\"), a Kōdōkan judoka who fought around the world, settled in **Belém, Brazil (1914–)**. He taught **Carlos Gracie** and others.\n- The **Gracie family** — Carlos, and especially **Hélio Gracie**, a lighter man — adapted techniques for leverage over strength, emphasising the guard and ground fighting. **Luiz França** and **Oswaldo Fadda** also spread the art outside the Gracie family.\n- \"Gracie challenges\" and vale tudo bouts proved the method; **Royce Gracie** won **UFC 1 (1993)**, sparking worldwide interest.\n- **IBJJF** was founded in 1994 (by Carlos Gracie Jr.) and runs the Worlds (Mundials), Pans and Europeans.\n\n## 2. Philosophy & etiquette\n\n- **Position before submission.** Control first, finish second.\n- **Leverage beats strength** — a smaller person can control a larger one with technique.\n- Tap = stop. Respect the tap instantly; tap early when caught.\n- Bow to the mat, slap-and-bump hands before rolling, keep nails short and gi clean, cover skin infections.\n\n## 3. Uniform & modest dressing\n\n**Gi (kimono):** heavy jacket with thick collar (long sleeves), reinforced trousers, belt. For modesty: rash guard (long-sleeve) and spats under the gi; **sports hijab** is widely accepted (IBJJF has approved hijab rules for athletes — check the current uniform rules). No-gi: long-sleeve rash guard + spats + shorts over them. In the demo the partner wears an open-face hijab; all figures fully covered, with smooth featureless skin-tone faces.\n\n## 4. Belts (IBJJF adult)\n\n| Belt | Typical time | Expectation |\n|---|---|---|\n| **White** (4 stripes) | 1–2 yrs | Survive, escape, basic positions & submissions |\n| **Blue** | 2–3 yrs | Fundamentals across all positions, a guard game |\n| **Purple** | 1.5–2+ yrs | Fluent transitions, own game, teaching beginners |\n| **Brown** | 1–2+ yrs | Refinement, high-level details |\n| **Black** (degrees 1–6) | ~8–12 yrs total | Mastery; **red-black** (7th), **red-white** (8th), **red** (9th–10th) |\n\nKids: grey, yellow, orange, green (each with white/black variants), up to age 15.\n\n## 5. Positional hierarchy\n\n```\nBack control  >  Mount  >  Knee-on-belly  >  Side control  >  Guard (top/bottom)  >  Turtle / standing\n```\n\n| Position | Goal (top) | Goal (bottom) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Closed guard** | Posture, open & pass | Break posture, sweep, submit (armbar, triangle, kimura) |\n| **Open guards** | Pass | Sweep, retain (butterfly, De La Riva, spider…) |\n| **Side control** | Mount, knee-on-belly, submit | Frame, shrimp, recover guard |\n| **Mount** | Submit (armbar, cross-collar choke) | Upa (bridge & roll), elbow-knee escape |\n| **Back control** | Rear naked/bow-and-arrow choke | Turn in, clear hooks |\n\n## 6. IBJJF points (gi)\n\nTakedown **2** · Sweep **2** · Knee-on-belly **2** · Guard pass **3** · Mount **4** · Back control (with hooks) **4**. Positions must be held 3 s. Advantages and penalties break ties; a submission wins outright.\n\n## 7. Core techniques by level\n\n| Level | Must-have techniques |\n|---|---|\n| White | Shrimp, bridge, technical stand-up, closed guard, scissor sweep, hip-bump sweep, armbar/triangle/kimura from guard, upa & elbow-knee escapes, side-control escape, RNC |\n| Blue | Toreando & knee-slice passes, half guard, butterfly guard, collar chokes, back takes |\n| Purple+ | Leg entanglements (by ruleset), advanced guards, chain attacks |\n\n## 8. Strategy\n\n1. **Frames and hips** — never fight from flat on your back; turn to the side and shrimp.\n2. **Grip fighting** — kill the opponent's grips before they kill yours.\n3. **Chain attacks**: armbar ↔ triangle ↔ omoplata from guard.\n4. **Pressure passing** vs **speed passing** (toreando) — mix them.\n5. Keep elbows tight on bottom to defend submissions.\n6. Win positions on points, finish when they're tired.\n\n## 9. Strengths & limitations\n\n| Strengths | Limitations |\n|---|---|\n| Proven ground control & submissions vs larger opponents | No striking in sport BJJ |\n| Live sparring (rolling) every class | Pulling guard is a risk vs multiple attackers / on hard ground |\n| Low impact on the head — trainable for life | Takedowns often under-trained |\n\n## 10. Training plan\n\n| Class (90 min) | Content |\n|---|---|\n| Warm-up 15 | Shrimping, bridging, forward/back rolls, sit-throughs |\n| Technique 30 | 2–3 techniques of one position, 10+ reps each per side |\n| Positional sparring 20 | Start in the position taught, 3 min rounds |\n| Free rolling 25 | 5×5 min |\n| Solo/home | Mobility, grip & core strength, review notes |\n\n3×/week gets a white belt to blue in about 2 years.\n\n## 11. Tips\n\n- Tap early, tap often — ego costs elbows.\n- Breathe and relax under pressure; panic burns energy.\n- Learn escapes first — defence earns time to learn offence.\n- Keep a training journal of positions you got stuck in.\n\n\n## 13. Glossary\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Rolling | Live sparring |\n| Guard / Pass / Sweep | Bottom with legs controlling / getting past the legs / reversing from bottom |\n| Hooks | Heels inside the opponent's thighs (back control) |\n| Oss | Greeting / acknowledgement |\n| Tap | Submit by tapping partner or mat |\n", "10-aikido": "# 10 · Aikido (合気道) — \"The Way of Harmonising Energy\"\n\n> **Origin:** Japan, 1920s–1940s · **Founder:** Morihei Ueshiba (*Ō-sensei*) · **Type:** blending, joint locks, throws, pins; weapons (bokken, jō, tantō)\n> **Main bodies:** Aikikai Foundation (Tokyo); also Yoshinkan, Shodokan/Tomiki (competitive), Ki-Aikido, Iwama-ryū\n\n---\n\n## 1. History & background\n\n- **Morihei Ueshiba (1883–1969)** trained in several jūjutsu and weapon arts, most importantly **Daitō-ryū Aiki-jūjutsu** under **Takeda Sōkaku** (from 1915).\n- Influenced by the **Ōmoto-kyō** spiritual movement, he transformed his martial art into a path of personal development and reconciliation. The name **Aikidō** became established in **1942**.\n- The **Kobukai** (1940) became the **Aikikai Foundation** (recognised 1948). Ueshiba's son **Kisshōmaru** and grandson **Moriteru** succeeded him as *Dōshu*.\n- Senior students founded influential branches: **Yoshinkan** (Gozo Shioda, strong basics, police training), **Shodokan/Tomiki** (with competition), **Ki Society** (Koichi Tohei), **Iwama** (Morihiro Saito, weapons integration).\n\n## 2. Philosophy & etiquette\n\n- **Ai** (harmony) – **Ki** (energy) – **Dō** (way): resolve conflict without destroying the attacker.\n- **Masakatsu agatsu** — \"true victory is victory over oneself.\"\n- Most Aikikai dojos have **no competition**; practice is cooperative, uke and nage both learning.\n- Za-rei to the *kamiza* and to the teacher, \"Onegaishimasu\" before, \"Dōmo arigatō gozaimashita\" after.\n\n## 3. Uniform & modest dressing\n\n**Keikogi** (white jacket, long sleeves; trousers) plus **hakama** — wide, pleated, ankle-length trousers that hide the shape of the legs. Traditionally hakama is worn from dan grade, but **many dojos allow women to wear hakama earlier** — an easy modest option. Add a fitted long-sleeve undershirt and a sports hijab/cap. In the demo, nage wears a dark hakama and an open-face hijab; ukes wear white gi and have black hair; all faces are smooth with no features.\n\n## 4. Ranks (Aikikai example)\n\n| Grade | Typical requirements |\n|---|---|\n| 5th–4th kyū | Ukemi, tai-sabaki, ikkyō, shihō-nage, irimi-nage, kote-gaeshi from basic grabs/strikes |\n| 3rd–1st kyū | Nikyō–yonkyō, kaiten-nage, kokyū-nage, suwari-waza (kneeling), hanmi-handachi |\n| Shodan | All of the above + **randori** vs multiple attackers, weapons taking (tantō/jō/tachi-dori) |\n| 2nd dan+ | Depth, freedom, teaching |\n\nBelts: usually white for kyū and black for dan (coloured belts for children in many dojos).\n\n## 5. Foundations\n\n| Skill | Description |\n|---|---|\n| **Kamae (hanmi)** | Triangular stance, hips and hands on the centre line |\n| **Tai-sabaki** | *Irimi* (entering) and *tenkan* (180° pivot) — the core movements |\n| **Ukemi** | Forward/backward rolls; high breakfalls for kote-gaeshi |\n| **Ma-ai** | Correct distance and timing |\n| **Atemi** | Strikes used to unbalance/occupy, mostly implied |\n\n## 6. Techniques\n\n**Attacks (kōgeki):** shōmen-uchi (overhead strike), yokomen-uchi (side strike), tsuki (punch), katate-dori (wrist grab), ryōte-dori, morote-dori, kata-dori, ushiro ryōte-dori.\n\n**Pins (osae-waza):** ikkyō, nikyō, sankyō, yonkyō, gokyō.\n**Throws (nage-waza):** shihō-nage, irimi-nage, kote-gaeshi, kaiten-nage, tenchi-nage, kokyū-nage, koshi-nage, jūji-garami.\n**Variations:** *omote* (entering in front) / *ura* (turning behind).\n\n## 7. Strategy & principles in application\n\n1. **Don't clash** — get off the line, join the attack's direction.\n2. **Take the balance first** (*kuzushi*) — a technique only works on an unbalanced uke.\n3. **Lead with the centre (hara)**, not the arms; the hands just connect.\n4. **Extend and relax** — tension tells uke what you're doing.\n5. **Randori:** keep moving, throw one uke into the path of the next, never stand between two.\n\n## 8. Strengths & limitations\n\n| Strengths | Limitations |\n|---|---|\n| Excellent falling skills & body awareness | Little live, resisting sparring in most dojos |\n| Joint-lock knowledge, weapons integration | Strikes are underdeveloped for sport/self-defence |\n| Lifelong, low-impact, ethical practice | Techniques depend on realistic, committed attacks |\n\n## 9. Training plan\n\n| Class (75–90 min) | Content |\n|---|---|\n| Aiki-taisō & warm-up | 10 min — wrist stretches, funekogi, tenkan practice |\n| Ukemi | 10 min — rolls both sides, breakfalls |\n| Technique | 40 min — one attack, 3–4 techniques, omote & ura, change partner |\n| Weapons | 15 min (some classes) — bokken suburi, jō |\n| Randori / jiyū-waza | 5–10 min |\n| Closing | Za-rei |\n\n## 10. Tips\n\n- Move your feet first; the arm movement comes from the body turn.\n- Keep your hands in front of your centre — \"between you and the work\".\n- As uke, give a sincere attack and protect yourself with good ukemi.\n- Weapon practice (bokken) sharpens the straight cut that ikkyō and shihō-nage use.\n\n\n## 12. Glossary\n\n| Japanese | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Nage / Uke | Thrower / attacker-receiver |\n| Omote / Ura | Front / back variation |\n| Suwari-waza / Hanmi-handachi | Both kneeling / nage kneeling, uke standing |\n| Bokken / Jō / Tantō | Wooden sword / staff / knife |\n| Randori | Free practice vs several attackers |\n"}};
